      "text": "Protection Agency (EPA) announced its National Primary Drinking Water Regulation (NPDWR) that set enforceable Maximum Contaminant Levels (MCLs) for six perfluoroalkyl acids (PFAA) including PFOA, PFOS, and PFBS [14] . Because of these new regulatory standards, remediation of PFAA-impacted water is an area of active research and innovation. Adsorption to activated carbon (AC) is often used to remove PFAS from surface water [2,15] . For PFAA, dual hydrophobic and electrostatic interactions are the thermodynamic driving forces, stemming from the perfluorinated alkyl chain and anionic head group, respectively. To remove PFAS from groundwater, ex situ pump and treat systems with either AC or anion exchange resins are often used [9,15-17] . One emerging in situ approach is enhanced attenuation through injection of adsorptive materials [15,18-24] . Often, colloidal activated carbon (CAC) is suspended in an anionic delivery polymer, such as carbox - ymethyl cellulose (CMC), and is injected under low pressure to form a permeable adsorptive barrier. When placed within a source and down - gradient of the resulting plume, the injected CAC slows PFAS migration and reduces downgradient mass flux, thereby lowering the risk of exposure [19,22,23,25] . Like ex situ applications, in situ CAC will"
